ance 35 mm) because a beam travelling in 0 deg hitting BS (angle_deg=0) reflects to +90 deg (up into M1, then right through PBS1 as H polarization), whereas the previous placement put BD_pump_r at (390,165) directly blocking the main branch from PBS1 to M1."}, {"op": "move", "element_id": "BD_pump_r", "field": "distance_mm", "from": 40.0, "to": 30.0, "note": "distance_mm: 40 \u2192 30 \u2014 Moved onto branch IN.r.t.r.r.t (distance 35 mm) because a beam travelling in 0 deg hitting BS (angle_deg=0) reflects to +90 deg (up into M1, then right through PBS1 as H polarization), whereas the previous placement put BD_pump_r at (390,165) directly blocking the main branch from PBS1 to M1."}, {"op": "param", "element_id": "BD_pump_r", "field": "aperture_mm", "from": 4.0, "to": null, "note": "aperture_mm: 4 \u2192 None \u2014 Moved onto branch IN.r.t.r.r.t (distance 35 mm) because a beam travelling in 0 deg hitting BS (angle_deg=0) reflects to +90 deg (up into M1, then right through PBS1 as H polarization), whereas the previous placement put BD_pump_r at (390,165) directly blocking the main branch from PBS1 to M1."}], "rationale": "BD_pump_r was mistakenly placed at (390, 165) on the PBS1->M1 segment because the counter-propagating pump actually reflects UP (+90 deg) at BS and then RIGHT (0 deg) at M1 before being transmitted through PBS1, and the BS->M1 spacing was only 35 mm causing mount/beam collisions; I increased the BS distance from M1 to 40 mm (with M4 at 80 mm to keep y=45) and placed a single dump BD_pump_r on IN.r.t.r.r.t after PBS1 to catch the returning H-polarized pump reflection cleanly."}

